Interface is a list of methods and we can define all the methods in there that you need in order to do something specific
for example: Assume you have a child that roams in a world and you want him to be able to lift anything that has the attachTo method
So you define:
interface Carriable {
void attachTo(Child child);
void unAttach();
}

Interfaces are public by default,
when you implement @Override is the best practice

Notes: You can't create instances of the interface! and usually he doesn't actually contain code.
the interface just defines what method an object needs to have so we could be able to lift him.
also the class has to officially declare that she implements the interface
Overall interface is like a contract on a class, ALL the methods in the interface have to be public!!!

so in the class definition we do:
class FurFly implements Carriable {
    public void attachTo(){
    }
    public void unAttach(){
    }
}
The class won't compile if it will not contain the attachTo, and unAttach methods.


